1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,377
What if the price of one tiny

2
00:00:02,377 --> 00:00:05,163
part inside your computer jumped more than

3
00:00:05,163 --> 00:00:07,418
five times in a single year,

4
00:00:07,418 --> 00:00:10,458
and almost nobody outside the industry

5
00:00:10,458 --> 00:00:12,071
noticed why?

6
00:00:17,317 --> 00:00:20,187
That part is a memory chip.

7
00:00:20,437 --> 00:00:22,662
In October twenty twenty-five,

8
00:00:22,662 --> 00:00:24,910
big buyers paid eight dollars and seventy

9
00:00:24,910 --> 00:00:27,158
cents for one standard sixteen gigabit

10
00:00:27,158 --> 00:00:28,657
memory chip.

11
00:00:28,887 --> 00:00:31,304
By September twenty twenty-six,

12
00:00:31,304 --> 00:00:33,857
the same chip cost forty-eight dollars.

13
00:00:33,947 --> 00:00:35,531
That is a rise of about four

14
00:00:35,531 --> 00:00:37,517
hundred and fifty percent.

15
00:00:37,687 --> 00:00:40,158
And behind that price are just three

16
00:00:40,158 --> 00:00:41,363
companies,

17
00:00:41,363 --> 00:00:43,507
making one quiet choice.

18
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:52,093
So what exactly got so expensive,

19
00:00:52,093 --> 00:00:53,982
and how much of it is in

20
00:00:53,982 --> 00:00:56,202
the device on your desk?

21
00:00:58,150 --> 00:01:00,700
The chip is called DRAM.

22
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:03,047
It is the working memory of every

23
00:01:03,047 --> 00:01:03,809
computer,

24
00:01:03,809 --> 00:01:04,181
phone,

25
00:01:04,181 --> 00:01:06,150
game console and server.

26
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:08,177
It holds whatever the machine is thinking

27
00:01:08,177 --> 00:01:09,510
about right now.

28
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:10,554
Without it,

29
00:01:10,554 --> 00:01:11,950
nothing runs.

30
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:13,776
And it is a commodity,

31
00:01:13,776 --> 00:01:14,954
sold by the billions,

32
00:01:14,954 --> 00:01:17,300
one tiny chip after another.

33
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:19,980
Most of it is sold under contracts.

34
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:20,635
Big buyers,

35
00:01:20,635 --> 00:01:22,646
like computer and phone makers,

36
00:01:22,646 --> 00:01:25,358
agree a price with the chipmaker every

37
00:01:25,358 --> 00:01:26,410
few months.

38
00:01:26,530 --> 00:01:28,393
Anyone without a contract,

39
00:01:28,393 --> 00:01:30,279
or who needs more than the contract

40
00:01:30,279 --> 00:01:30,963
covers,

41
00:01:30,963 --> 00:01:32,590
buys on the spot market,

42
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:35,076
at whatever price the day brings.

43
00:01:35,317 --> 00:01:38,257
Take one ordinary sixteen gigabyte memory

44
00:01:38,257 --> 00:01:38,987
stick.

45
00:01:38,987 --> 00:01:41,457
Inside it sit eight of those chips.

46
00:01:41,457 --> 00:01:43,509
In October twenty twenty-five,

47
00:01:43,509 --> 00:01:44,959
the chips on that stick cost big

48
00:01:44,959 --> 00:01:47,217
buyers about seventy dollars.

49
00:01:47,247 --> 00:01:48,998
By September twenty twenty-six,

50
00:01:48,998 --> 00:01:51,367
about three hundred and eighty dollars.

51
00:01:51,467 --> 00:01:53,125
And that is only the chips,

52
00:01:53,125 --> 00:01:54,783
before anyone builds the stick,

53
00:01:54,783 --> 00:01:56,252
ships it or puts it on a

54
00:01:56,252 --> 00:01:57,061
shelf.

55
00:01:57,317 --> 00:01:59,126
The worst jump came at the start

56
00:01:59,126 --> 00:02:01,277
of twenty twenty-six.

57
00:02:01,347 --> 00:02:03,371
The research firm TrendForce expected

58
00:02:03,371 --> 00:02:05,846
contract prices for PC memory to rise

59
00:02:05,846 --> 00:02:07,645
by more than double in just three

60
00:02:07,645 --> 00:02:08,073
months,

61
00:02:08,073 --> 00:02:10,322
the largest quarterly price surge it had

62
00:02:10,322 --> 00:02:11,667
ever recorded.

63
00:02:11,667 --> 00:02:14,099
Computer makers that had signed supply

64
00:02:14,099 --> 00:02:16,167
deals watched their stockpiles shrink.

65
00:02:16,167 --> 00:02:17,287
Those without deals had to pay whatever

66
00:02:17,287 --> 00:02:18,447
the market asked.

67
00:02:18,637 --> 00:02:20,273
Server memory,

68
00:02:20,273 --> 00:02:22,465
the kind data centers buy,

69
00:02:22,465 --> 00:02:26,070
was expected to jump about ninety percent

70
00:02:26,070 --> 00:02:28,057
in the same quarter.

71
00:02:28,147 --> 00:02:30,413
And the biggest cloud companies in North

72
00:02:30,413 --> 00:02:33,132
America and China were negotiating

73
00:02:33,132 --> 00:02:35,544
long-term deals to lock in supply.

74
00:02:35,783 --> 00:02:37,909
A price can only move like that

75
00:02:37,909 --> 00:02:41,433
if very few people control the supply.

76
00:02:41,453 --> 00:02:43,036
So who are they?

77
00:02:48,683 --> 00:02:51,717
Who actually makes the world's memory?

78
00:02:57,683 --> 00:03:00,255
Almost all of it comes from three

79
00:03:00,255 --> 00:03:01,713
companies.

80
00:03:01,823 --> 00:03:04,178
In the second quarter of twenty

81
00:03:04,178 --> 00:03:05,199
twenty-six,

82
00:03:05,199 --> 00:03:09,699
Samsung took 39.4% of all DRAM sales,

83
00:03:09,699 --> 00:03:16,213
SK hynix 24.9% and Micron 23.3%.

84
00:03:16,353 --> 00:03:17,229
Together,

85
00:03:17,229 --> 00:03:19,701
almost nine in every ten dollars spent

86
00:03:19,701 --> 00:03:22,323
on memory went to just these three.

87
00:03:22,753 --> 00:03:25,180
The whole market reached about one hundred

88
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:27,607
and fifty-five billion dollars in a single

89
00:03:27,607 --> 00:03:28,194
quarter,

90
00:03:28,194 --> 00:03:30,418
up almost sixty percent from the quarter

91
00:03:30,418 --> 00:03:31,423
before.

92
00:03:31,863 --> 00:03:33,973
And look at how the order moved.

93
00:03:34,233 --> 00:03:36,924
SK hynix sells the most AI memory,

94
00:03:36,924 --> 00:03:38,874
so its prices rose the least,

95
00:03:38,874 --> 00:03:42,404
and its share fell from 28.8% to

96
00:03:42,404 --> 00:03:45,053
24.9%.

97
00:03:45,353 --> 00:03:46,008
Samsung,

98
00:03:46,008 --> 00:03:48,040
which started mass production of the

99
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:50,050
newest AI memory early,

100
00:03:50,050 --> 00:03:52,005
pulled further ahead.

101
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:54,188
Let me draw why three is such

102
00:03:54,188 --> 00:03:56,450
a powerful number.

103
00:03:56,470 --> 00:03:59,273
A memory factory costs billions and takes

104
00:03:59,273 --> 00:04:00,500
years to build.

105
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:02,675
So nobody new can jump in when

106
00:04:02,675 --> 00:04:04,000
prices rise.

107
00:04:04,010 --> 00:04:06,006
And when only three sellers exist,

108
00:04:06,006 --> 00:04:07,619
none of them has to agree with

109
00:04:07,619 --> 00:04:09,630
the others on anything.

110
00:04:09,670 --> 00:04:11,680
Each one simply looks at its own

111
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:13,991
order book and asks one question:

112
00:04:13,991 --> 00:04:16,251
which customer pays the most for the

113
00:04:16,251 --> 00:04:17,856
same silicon?

114
00:04:18,117 --> 00:04:20,066
In twenty twenty-six,

115
00:04:20,066 --> 00:04:22,609
the answer to that question was not

116
00:04:22,609 --> 00:04:23,491
you.

117
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:31,512
Who is outbidding the rest of us

118
00:04:31,512 --> 00:04:33,162
for memory?

119
00:04:38,150 --> 00:04:40,200
Artificial intelligence.

120
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:41,940
The chips that train and run AI

121
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:43,654
models need a special,

122
00:04:43,654 --> 00:04:47,109
extremely fast kind of memory called HBM,

123
00:04:47,109 --> 00:04:48,823
high bandwidth memory,

124
00:04:48,823 --> 00:04:51,660
stacked right next to the processor.

125
00:04:51,970 --> 00:04:54,180
It sells for far more.

126
00:04:54,370 --> 00:04:56,020
But it is greedy.

127
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:57,564
According to Micron,

128
00:04:57,564 --> 00:05:00,026
making HBM takes about three times the

129
00:05:00,026 --> 00:05:02,936
wafers that ordinary DDR five memory needs

130
00:05:02,936 --> 00:05:05,550
for the same amount of memory.

131
00:05:06,020 --> 00:05:08,443
And Micron expects the market for HBM

132
00:05:08,443 --> 00:05:11,108
alone to reach one hundred billion dollars

133
00:05:11,108 --> 00:05:12,920
by twenty twenty-eight.

134
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:14,704
That is a forecast,

135
00:05:14,704 --> 00:05:16,718
but it tells you where the factories

136
00:05:16,718 --> 00:05:17,990
want to go.

137
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:19,943
Let me draw what that means for

138
00:05:19,943 --> 00:05:21,510
the factory.

139
00:05:21,650 --> 00:05:24,040
Imagine one silicon wafer.

140
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:26,214
Cut it into ordinary memory,

141
00:05:26,214 --> 00:05:27,797
and you get a certain amount of

142
00:05:27,797 --> 00:05:29,908
capacity to sell to computer and phone

143
00:05:29,908 --> 00:05:30,860
makers.

144
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:33,388
Turn the same wafer into HBM,

145
00:05:33,388 --> 00:05:35,701
and you get only about a third

146
00:05:35,701 --> 00:05:37,218
as much memory,

147
00:05:37,218 --> 00:05:39,018
but each bit sells for a lot

148
00:05:39,018 --> 00:05:41,160
more to AI companies.

149
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:43,993
Every wafer moved to HBM is a

150
00:05:43,993 --> 00:05:46,777
wafer taken away from the memory in

151
00:05:46,777 --> 00:05:48,073
your laptop.

152
00:05:48,317 --> 00:05:50,647
Some companies went further.

153
00:05:50,787 --> 00:05:53,097
In December twenty twenty-five,

154
00:05:53,097 --> 00:05:55,430
Micron announced it was leaving its

155
00:05:55,430 --> 00:05:57,040
Crucial consumer brand,

156
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:59,374
the memory sticks and drives sold in

157
00:05:59,374 --> 00:05:59,817
shops,

158
00:05:59,817 --> 00:06:02,127
to improve supply for its larger,

159
00:06:02,127 --> 00:06:04,127
strategic customers.

160
00:06:04,627 --> 00:06:06,931
Crucial products shipped until the end of

161
00:06:06,931 --> 00:06:09,207
February twenty twenty-six.

162
00:06:09,527 --> 00:06:11,465
And Micron said that even then,

163
00:06:11,465 --> 00:06:13,642
it expected to meet only half to

164
00:06:13,642 --> 00:06:15,602
two-thirds of what its key customers

165
00:06:15,602 --> 00:06:16,637
